  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Triple NHS investment with 18‑week treatment targets, smoke‑free workplaces, restrictions on junk‑food marketing to children, and expanded cessation services; halve child poverty by 2010 and end it in a generation via work, tax credits and Child Trust Fund; universal childcare through Sure Start and extended schools; expanded flexible working (including carers); Pension Credit and Winter Fuel Payments; establish a Single Equality Act and an Equality & Human Rights Commission. TEXT_F: 5,000 extra police per year, 20,000 extra prison places, end early release, elected police commissioners; controlled immigration with a Border Control Police and annual immigration limit; tax cuts and deregulation to ‘reward hard work’; NHS infection control and more single rooms; scrap university tuition fees; halve council tax for over‑65s and restore earnings link to the basic state pension.",
  "reasoning": "Given the electorate’s highest weight on Care, with moderate Authority/Loyalty and lower Equality/Proportionality, TEXT_E aligns better by centering broad pro‑social, health, and anti‑poverty measures alongside equality infrastructure. TEXT_F places comparatively greater emphasis on Authority/Loyalty and Proportionality (policing, prisons, immigration limits, tax cuts), which are less prioritized in the profile, despite some Care policies.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
